NAME
    tail - print the trailing part of files

SYNOPSIS
    tail [-c COUNT] [-n COUNT] [-qv] [FILE...]
    tail -COUNT [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the last 10 lines of each FILE. With no file, reads standard input.
    A FILE of - also selects standard input. At an interactive prompt that
    reads typed lines until Ctrl-D; see man sh.

    -c COUNT  Print the last COUNT bytes instead of lines.
    -n COUNT  Print the last COUNT lines. -COUNT is the same request.
    -n +COUNT Start at line COUNT, counting from one.
    -c +COUNT Start at byte COUNT, counting from one.
    -q        Never print the ==> FILE <== header.
    -v        Always print the header, even for a single operand.

    A named file is read backwards from its end, so the trailing lines of a
    large file cost their own size rather than the file's. A pipe or the
    session terminal cannot be read backwards, so at most 8 KiB is retained;
    a request beyond that is reported rather than silently truncated. Counting
    from the start with +COUNT streams and retains nothing.

    There is no -f: the namespace has no change-notification interface, so a
    follow mode could only poll.

    Every operand is attempted; a failure is reported and the remaining
    operands still run. A missing file exits 3, other failures exit 1.
